378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 770 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables. This means we give a name to a pin number so it is easy to use in the code. For example, if we write int ledPin = 5; and int buttonPin = 2;, it means we are using those pins and giving them simple names.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [31]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 771 0 R
/E (Next comes the setup\(\) function. This part runs only one time when the Arduino is turned on. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell the Arduino whether a pin will work as an input or an output. For example, the LED pin is set as OUTPUT and the button pin is set as INPUT.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [32]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 772 0 R
/E (After that comes the loop\(\) function. This part runs again and again forever, so the Arduino keeps checking or controlling the circuit continuously.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [33]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 773 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, we use digitalRead\(\) to check the state of a button, switch, or sensor. It tells us whether the input is HIGH \(pressed/on\) or LOW \(not pressed/off\). We can then use digitalWrite\(\) to turn an output like an LED ON or OFF.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
