474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 798 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables. This means we give a name to a pin number so it is easy to use in the code. For example, if we write int ledPin = 9;, it means we are using pin number 9 and calling it ledPin.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 799 0 R
/E (Next comes the setup\(\) function. This part runs only one time when the Arduino is turned on. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell the Arduino whether a pin will work as an input or an output. For example, setting a pin as OUTPUT allows it to control devices like an LED.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 800 0 R
/E (After that comes the loop\(\) function. This part runs again and again forever, so the Arduino keeps checking or controlling the circuit continuously.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 801 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, we can use analogWrite\(\) to control the brightness of an LED or the speed of a motor using PWM. The values range from 0 \(off\) to 255 \(full brightness\), with values in between giving different levels. We can also use a for loop to repeat the same action multiple times without writing the same code again and again, making the program shorter and easier to understand.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 802 0 R
/E (So overall, the program first prepares everything in setup\(\), then keeps running the loop continuously, controlling devices and repeating actions using functions like pinMode\(\), analogWrite\(\), and loops like for.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
